# Without this file, the Dockerfile's `COPY . .` sends *everything* in the
# build context into the image -- most importantly .env, which would bake
# real secrets (SECRET_KEY, DATABASE_URL, API keys) directly into an image
# layer. Anyone who ever pulls that image, even after the file is later
# "removed" in a later layer, can still extract it from the layer history.
# Real config comes from Railway's environment variables instead (see
# README.md's deployment notes) -- .env is strictly local-dev-only.

# Secrets -- never bake these into an image layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Python environments / caches -- huge, and irrelevant to the image (deps
# come from requirements.txt via pip inside the build instead).
.venv/
venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Git metadata -- large, and the image has no use for repo history.
.git/
.gitignore

# Local runtime data -- shouldn't ship whatever happens to exist on the
# machine running `docker build`; the container creates its own uploads/
# directory fresh (see Dockerfile's `RUN mkdir -p uploads`) and gets its own
# database from DATABASE_URL, not a stale local file.
uploads/*
!uploads/.gitkeep
test_uploads/
*.db

# Editor/OS cruft.
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/

# Dev-only docs -- not needed for the app to run.
README.md
